**Subject.** In many works studied this year, telling one's own story is not only a private matter.

> "Why can writing about oneself be a political act?"

Write a structured paragraph (about 150 words) that:
1. defines the idea in your own words;
2. uses at least **two** precise references from the course (author, title, date), chosen among the *voices from the margins* or the *speeches that shaped history*;
3. uses at least two logical connectors (*however*, *therefore*, *whereas*…) and one key notion of the chapter (*voice*, *memoir*, *testimony*, *empowerment*, *double consciousness*…).

**Document (invented interview).**

> "My grandparents changed their name when they arrived in America. They stopped speaking their language at home, because they wanted their children to be 'real Americans'. My parents made the opposite choice: we speak two languages, we cook the dishes of our grandparents, and I have never felt less American for it." — Daniela, 17

**Task.** In about 150 words:
1. explain the two metaphors used to describe what becomes of migrants in America (*melting pot* and *salad bowl*): what each one asserts and what it assumes;
2. show which generation of Daniela's family illustrates each metaphor;
3. give your own opinion, using at least one reference from the course (Ellis Island, or a novel about the second generation).
